  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening TAC to engage with government to deliver quality HIV services in S</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In South Africa, more than 5 million people live with HIV, and 1,000 people are infected every day. Most of the 1.5 million South Africans in need of antiretroviral therapy (ART) are not receiving it. In the past, South Africa&apos;s political leaders were unwilling to address HIV at a level proportionate to the crisis. During these difficult years, Treatment Action Campaign (TAC) fought for, amongst other things, the right to treatment for people with HIV. In 2008, with a change in government, there was a change in policy on access to treatment and the government now fully supports this work.  As a result, TAC has changed its strategy and is now a &#x2018;critical friend&apos; of government, supporting implementation of the National Strategic Plan for the prevention and treatment of HIV.  This investment grant will enable TAC to make four step changes&apos; in its work. This includes getting up to four million more people to test for HIV, making sure 500,000 on ART know how to stay healthy, and getting 200,000 pregnant women to take the necessary drugs to prevent transmission of the virus to their babies.  TAC will also promote safe sex, support women to get treatment and those suffering rape, abuse or domestic violence to get legal, health and counselling services. TAC, as an organisation led by positive people, will scale up their work to tackle stigma and discrimination. They will also invest in their own organisation, particularly in relation to governance, staff development, communications, and monitoring and evaluation, so they become more effective.  As a result, more people (including children) with advanced HIV infection will receive ART, more infections will be prevented and 80% of people with HIV will have access to services that will help them live longer and healthier lives.</narrative>
  </description>
